"Untitled Document (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22511v1)\nciteturn41209view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn41196view3\",\"lineno\":410}); Total lines: 1079\nL354: Figure 2: Episode-level agent evaluation separates verified outcomes from trace diagnostics. Dashed arrows mark caller-supplied artifact links. Listing 6: Episode evaluation: a final-state oracle is the headline; tool scores are diagnostics; nothing is called.\nL355: \nL356: ⬇\nL357: \nL358: from auditkit.agent_eval import (AgentCase, AgentEvalRunner, AgentEvalSpec,\nL359: \nL360: FinalStateAssertion, episode_from_openai_messages)\nL361: \nL362: episode = episode_from_openai_messages(messages, case_id=\"inventory-001\",\nL363: \nL364: final_answer=\"Shipment for A is ready.\")\nL365: episode.final_state = {\"shipment_decision\": \"ready\"} # the verified effect\nL366: \nL367: case = AgentCase(id=\"inventory-001\", task=\"Ship item A if it is in stock.\",\nL368: \nL369: allowed_tools=[\"lookup_inventory\"],\nL370: \nL371: outcome=FinalStateAssertion(\"shipment_decision\", equals=\"ready\"))\nL372: \nL373: result = AgentEvalRunner().run(AgentEvalSpec(\nL374: \nL375: cases=[case], mode=\"recorded\", episodes=[episode], trials=1,\nL376: \nL377: scorers=[\"tool_call_f1\", \"tool_call_validity\"])) # add judge=... for task_completion\nL378: # Verified success: 1/1 (1/1 decided) Failure: 0 Unknown outcome: 0 ...\nL379: ### 7.4  Retrieval-augmented generation\nL380: A RAG pipeline is scored on what it actually retrieved: the trace’s contexts when returned, the dataset’s otherwise. RetrievalMetrics(k) is deterministic; its average precision divides by all relevant items, so an unretrieved gold chunk lowers the score, and its ideal DCG [cite124†15 ] comes from the gold grades. Judged metrics work with any text judge and treat an unparseable verdict as an error; faithfulness fails unsupported claims, not only contradicted ones (Listing cite125†18 ).\nL381: In regulated settings such as banking, a grounded answer can still rest on a stale, unauthorized, or misattributed source.\nL382: Seven model-free checks read a versioned CorpusSnapshot, a RAGCase (sufficient evidence sets, citations, tenant, decision date), and a RAGTrace, and each returns a score or an explicit unknown: a complete evidence set was retrieved and kept in the final context, the documents were current at the decision date, retrieval stayed within the caller’s access rights, citations exist and support the answer, the numbers are right, and the system abstained exactly when the question was unanswerable.\nL383: ### 7.5  Integration with external artifacts\nL384: AuditKIT reads AgentTune [cite73†28 ] artifacts by record shape, never by import, and sets coverage from what each artifact contains (Table cite126†5 ); a serialized EventLog’s turn boundaries become parallel groups, and AgentTune’s healing-audit records, a per-call projection rather than a trajectory, are not imported.\nL385: Several AgentTune metrics map onto AuditKIT in stricter form: argument correctness to tool_call_validity, redundancy ratio to redundant_tool_calls, loop frequency to agent_loop_detection, tool-chain order to trajectory_in_order, and message-id recall to retrieval recall; self-correction, tool efficacy, plan adherence, and path edit distance have no counterpart yet.\nL386: AuditKIT never writes into AgentTune’s data: write_sidecar() emits verdicts, coverage, and reliability keyed by trajectory id (Listing cite127†19 ).\nL387: Table 5: AgentTune artifact fields and the diagnostics available from them.\nL388: AgentTune artifact AuditKIT entry point What is observed Eligible without annotation Evaluation / RL dataset rows (prompt, gold answer, gold chunk ids) load_agenttune a task to run: input, target, gold contexts, and the original system prompt; no events yet answer metrics; retrieval once a live run returns ids; task_completion Trajectory JSONL (steps) load_agenttune, episodes_from_agenttune tool calls per step with arguments when recorded; conversation; retrieved chunk ids; reward as provenance redundancy, loop detection, task_completion; reference metrics once a reference is attached Training trace (trace.jsonl) same final answer and retrieved ids; calls carry no tool names answer metrics, retrieval, task_completion Evaluation report JSON same answers plus flattened tool names only (tool_names_only); report metadata kept tool_call_f1_name, tool_call_validity, task_completion Serialized EventLog episode_from_eventlog ordered events, turn grouping (full tier), call ids, reward as provenance all trajectory metrics the recorded tier supports Healing audit records refused (named error) a lossy per-call projection none\nL389: The same read-only pattern covers AlignTune [cite74†27 ] (scorecards and regression reports), SafeTune [cite75†40 ] (policy events and benchmark rows), CuratorKIT [cite76†2 ] (samples with provenance, manifests, trainer exports), and CircuitKIT [cite77†39 ] (circuit faithfulness reports). Each artifact is wrapped in a SourceEvidence envelope listing the join keys present and missing, and join_evidence() attaches it to an episode only through ids the caller names, refusing to pin aggregate evidence to one case.\nL390: ## 8  Empirical Studies\nL391: Each study is a single run or a small or controlled sample (the sample size $N$, from 6 to 100, is stated with each). They illustrate what the library reports and distinguishes rather than general model rankings. Studies 1 to 3 ran on the earlier auditkit 0.3.0 line (44 metrics, 7 adapters, no agent layer, not published on PyPI); Studies 4 to 7 used the 1.1.2 release, Study 4 through its vLLM and SGLang integration code.\nL392: All runs use greedy decoding, with output budgets (max_tokens) of 8 for Study 1, 300 for Study 2, 100 for Study 3 (200 for its judge), 64 for Study 5, and 512 for Study 7. Studies 1 to 3 cover arbitrary metrics, direction-correct multi-model comparison, and performance measurement. Study 4 serves one model family through four runtimes.\nL393: Studies 5 to 7 apply the RAG, interoperability, and tool-calling layers: a multi-hop question-answering assistant, artifacts from the Lexsi Labs stack, and the choice of a tool-calling model. Studies 5 and 6 are the smallest and illustrate what each layer reports.\nL394: ### 8.1  Study 1: Evaluating pruned models\nL395: We evaluate three community-pruned MLP-neuron variants of Llama-3.2-3B [cite128†10 ] (the third-party oopere/pruned{20,40,60}-llama-3.2-3b checkpoints, whose pruning method and any post-pruning recovery are the checkpoint author’s, not ours) against the unpruned base model on 100 BoolQ [cite129†5 ] questions, one run per severity and without repeated seeds. The baseline is the base (not instruct) model with a one-shot prompt, and the answer is a RegexAnnotator match on ^\\s*(yes|no).\nL396: Each variant is compared with the baseline through RunComparison.retention() (candidate $\\div$ baseline) on three metrics: boolq_answer_match (exact-match answer scoring), not_degenerate_output (flags only three identical consecutive words; it does not detect empty output), and factual_correctness_judge (LLMJudge-based).\nL397: Table 6: Relative metric retention by pruning severity. Baseline absolute scores were not retained, so the ratios are reported as relative retention only.\nL398: \nL399: Pruning severity boolq_answer_match ($\\uparrow$) not_degenerate_output ($\\uparrow$) factual_correctness_judge ($\\uparrow$) 20% 0.411 1.000 0.385 40% 0.726 1.000 0.788 60% 0.630 1.000 0.673\nL400: Retention is non-monotonic across severities, and every variant fails the predefined threshold. The flat non-degeneracy score is a coarse signal, because the output budget is short and the heuristic misses empty outputs. The baseline’s absolute scores were not retained and may be near chance, so the ratios describe relative retention rather than a pruning effect. Model size falls with severity, which confirms the pruning took effect.\nL401: ### 8.2  Study 2: Comparing language models\nL402: \nL403: We run compare_models() over three local Hugging Face checkpoints (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct [cite128†10 ], Llama-2-13b-chat-hf [cite130†11 ], Qwen/Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct [cite131†35 ]; via HFGenModel) on 100 SST-2 [cite132†41 ] sentiment samples with QuasiExactMatch, reporting per-model accuracy, latency, throughput, and model size (Table cite133†7 ).\nL404: \nL405: Table 7: Model scores and whole-run time on SST-2; run time is a whole-run measurement, not per-request latency.\nL406: Model quasi_exact_match ($\\uparrow$) Params Size (MiB) Run time (ms, $\\downarrow$) llama-8b (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct) 0.930 8.03B 15,316.5 6,601 llama-13b (Llama-2-13b-chat-hf) 0.580 13.02B 24,825.8 32,388 qwen-14b (Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct) 0.900 14.77B 28,171.6 106,512\nL407: Adding regex extraction changes the apparent ranking even though the generated outputs are unchanged: the model that scores lowest without extraction becomes competitive after its labels are parsed. This shows how answer formatting can masquerade as a model-quality difference.\nL408: Whole-run wall time is not per-request latency and varied substantially between runs, so it is not used for a speed ranking. Per-sample scores were not retained, so we reconstructed confidence intervals and significance tests from aggregate accuracies; the paired test that suits these repeated examples is unavailable. The post-extraction differences are not statistically significant on these aggregates, and equivalence of the models is not tested.\nL409: The study shows that generation, annotation, scoring, and performance measurement can be varied independently under one common RunResult.\nL410: Table 8: Scores after regex extraction; timings are whole-run measurements from a separate run.\nL411: \nL412: Model quasi_exact_match ($\\uparrow$) Std. dev. $n$ Run time (ms, $\\downarrow$, this run) llama-8b (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct) 0.930 (unchanged) 0.2551 100 64,861 llama-13b (Llama-2-13b-chat-hf) 0.910 (was 0.580) 0.2862 100 112,349 qwen-14b (Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct) 0.960 (was 0.900) 0.1960 100 107,349\nL413: ### 8.3  Study 3: Financial question answering\nL414: Financial-filing question answering needs the right number, not a plausible sentence, so numeric correctness and semantic grounding fail differently. We run compare_models() on 100 question/answer/context triplets from virattt/financial-qa-10K [cite134†44 ]; the first 100 rows are all NVIDIA (NVDA), so this is a single-company case study. It compares Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct [cite128†10 ] (fp16) with Llama-2-13b-chat-hf [cite130†11 ], judged by an independent Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct [cite128†10 ].\nL415: Four scorers run per sample: NumericFactMatch (a notebook-defined heuristic that checks whether the first extracted number equals the first reference number, and breaks on unit changes such as “$26.97 billion” vs “26,974 million”), LexicalGroundedness and BertScore (grounding and semantic similarity against the filing excerpt), and factual_correctness_judge.\nL416: The judge is in the same family as llama-8b, so a self-preference effect is possible. Llama-2-13b-chat-hf and the judge were loaded 4-bit quantized to fit beside the unquantized 8B model on one GPU. This confounds the 8B-vs-13B comparison, because quantization can affect accuracy and run time as well as size: llama-13b’s reported size, 6,675.8 MiB, is smaller than llama-8b’s fp16 15,316.5 MiB despite more parameters.\nL417: Table 9: Metric scores on questions from NVIDIA’s 2023 10-K filing. The 13B model and the judge ran 4-bit quantized, and numeric_fact_match compares only the first extracted number.\nL418: \nL419: Model numeric_fact_match ($\\uparrow$) lexical_groundedness ($\\uparrow$) bert_score ($\\uparrow$) factual_correctness_judge ($\\uparrow$) llama-8b (fp16) 0.3800 0.6659 0.8685 0.8300 llama-13b (4-bit) 0.4100 0.5812 0.7936 0.8700\nL420: The metrics disagree: one model leads on grounding, while the quantized larger model leads on numeric matching and the judge score. The latter gaps overlap their uncertainty intervals, so they are not used for a ranking. The case shows why metric disagreement and evaluation setup, including quantization and judge family, should be reported together.\nL421: ### 8.4  Study 4: Evaluation across serving runtimes\nL422: We run nine Cohere models [cite135†6 ] through four routes: hf:, offline vllm:, SGLang via api:, and the Hugging Face router via api:. Each model answers one factual question in five languages (PASS $\\geq$ 4/5), on a 40 GB A100 or 95 GB RTX PRO 6000. The vllm: column ran vLLM 0.30.0 with transformers 5.16.1, the SGLang column SGLang 0.5.20, and the hf: column transformers 5.17, measured between 2026-09-30 and 2026-10-01; all use greedy decoding.\nL423: Image tests in the vllm: column use vllm serve through api:; offline vllm: does not accept images. Superscripts mark Transformers backends, SGLang threshold reporting, or matching answers across routes.\nL424: Table 10: Correct answers out of five language probes by model and serving route. These are compatibility probes, not estimates of multilingual performance.\nL425: Model Architecture hf: vllm: SGLang via api: HF router via api: Tiny Aya Global cohere2 4/5 4/5 3/5^{⋆} text Tiny Aya Fire cohere2 3/5 4/5 3/5^{⋆} text Tiny Aya Water cohere2 4/5 4/5 3/5^{⋆} text Tiny Aya Earth cohere2 3/5 4/5 3/5^{⋆} text North Micro Vision Instruct cohere_compass 3/5 5/5, image PASS^{§‡}, image text only Aya Expanse 8B cohere 5/5 5/5 5/5 text Aya Expanse 32B cohere 5/5 5/5 5/5 text Aya Vision 8B aya_vision 5/5 5/5^{†}, image 5/5^{‡}, image text only Aya Vision 32B aya_vision 4/5 5/5^{†}, image PASS^{§‡}, image text only\nL426: A model can load across serving routes yet produce different evaluation outcomes. In one route, a special token left in the response causes an exact-match failure; another route strips it. This small probe checks runtime compatibility, not comparative model quality.\nL427: ### 8.5  Study 5: Retrieval and answer quality\nL428: A RAG assistant can fail in its retriever or in its generator, and the two need separate measurements. We answer six HotpotQA [cite136†46 ] distractor questions, each with ten candidate paragraphs of which two hold the supporting facts, using two retrievers, BM25 [cite137†36 ] and TF-IDF, that pass their top three paragraphs to the same generator (Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct).\nL429: Each sample carries the retrieved paragraphs as retrieval_context and the gold paragraphs as reference_contexts; RetrievalMetrics(k=3) scores what was retrieved, and quasi-exact match and token F1 score the answer. The prompt places the passages before the question.\nL430: Table 11: Retriever and answer metrics for HotpotQA ($N\\!=\\!6$, $k\\!=\\!3$, one shared generator).\nL431: Retriever  | Hit@3  | P@3  | R@3  | MRR  | nDCG@3  | QEM  | F1\nL432: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL433: BM25  | 0.833  | 0.500  | 0.750  | 0.750  | 0.704  | 0.333  | 0.370\nL434: TF-IDF  | 0.833  | 0.389  | 0.583  | 0.750  | 0.602  | 0.333  | 0.333\nL435: The retrievers tie on hit rate, but ranking-sensitive measures reveal differences in the retrieved evidence. Answer scores also change when the prompt places the question before rather than after the passages, while retrieval scores stay fixed. Six questions illustrate the measures rather than rank the retrievers; the case shows why retrieval and generation need separate measures.\nL436: ### 8.6  Study 6: Evaluating artifacts from the Lexsi Labs stack\nL437: \nL438: We test interoperability using sample artifacts from AgentTune (two trajectories), CuratorKIT, AlignTune, SafeTune, and CircuitKIT. The records are placeholders, so this study evaluates parsing and linking behavior, not model quality. An artifact-based link is checked against an identifier it contains; a caller-based link uses an identifier supplied externally.\nL439: Table 12: Evidence-linking outcomes for a sample AgentTune episode (constructed placeholder artifacts).\nL440: Evidence  | Granularity  | Ids given  | Result\nL441: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL442: AlignTune regression report  | aggregate  | artifact name, format  | joined (artifact)\nL443: AlignTune audit report  | aggregate  | checkpoint digest  | joined (caller)\nL444: CircuitKIT faithfulness report  | aggregate  | model digest  | joined (caller)\nL445: SafeTune audit event  | per event  | episode id, call id  | joined (caller)\nL446: SafeTune audit event  | per event  | decision only  | refused: describes, does not identify\nL447: CuratorKIT manifest  | aggregate  | row id  | refused: aggregate cannot pin one row\nL448: Final-state assertions verify the recorded episodes, while missing tool arguments and rewards remain unscored rather than inferred. Evidence records are joined only when their identifiers support the link; descriptive labels and aggregate manifests are refused when they cannot identify an episode.\nL449: ### 8.7  Study 7: Choosing a tool-calling model\nL450: An assistant that books, ships, or refunds must pick the right tool, fill its arguments, issue independent calls together, and recognize when no tool applies. A larger model costs more to serve, so we ask what the extra parameters buy.\nL451: We compare Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct [cite131†35 ] on the first 100 questions of five single-turn BFCL [cite87†31 ] categories: one call (simple), choosing among tools (multiple), independent calls in one response (parallel, parallel_multiple), and questions no tool answers (irrelevance). Both models run through vllm: with native tool schemas via ToolCallAdapter, and every call is scored with BFCL’s accepted-value rules (bfcl_arg_match).\nL452: Call F1 uses maximum bipartite matching; exact set requires every call and argument to match. Parallel precision and recall apply to categories with independent reference calls. Validity is averaged over questions with a tool call, redundancy is lower-is-better, and in irrelevance F1/exact-set measure correctly making no call.\nL453: Table 13: Tool-calling metrics across BFCL categories. Validity is averaged over questions with a tool call; parallel precision and recall apply only to categories with independent reference calls (n/a otherwise).\nL454: Category Model Call F1 Exact set Par. recall Par. precision Validity Redundant simple 7B 0.980 0.98 n/a n/a 1.000 0.000 14B 0.980 0.98 n/a n/a 1.000 0.000 multiple 7B 0.937 0.93 n/a n/a 1.000 0.000 14B 0.948 0.93 n/a n/a 1.000 0.000 parallel 7B 0.912 0.89 0.89 1.00 1.000 0.000 14B 0.919 0.89 0.89 1.00 0.993 0.000 parallel_multiple 7B 0.905 0.80 0.83 1.00 0.989 0.000 14B 0.897 0.81 0.84 1.00 0.989 0.012 irrelevance 7B 0.590 0.59 n/a n/a 0.732 0.000 14B 0.710 0.71 n/a n/a 0.828 0.017\nL455: The models perform similarly on categories that require a tool call, while the larger model more often declines irrelevant requests; uncertainty intervals overlap, so this difference is suggestive. On parallel calls, call-level F1 preserves partial credit that exact-set scoring hides, whereas parallel precision is uninformative with single-turn references. These results describe this benchmark slice.\nL456: ## 9  Limitations and Ethics Statement\nL457: AuditKIT is a measurement library, and several parts of it are deliberately modest in scope. Image inputs are supported without image-specific metrics, and simulated-user conversation, perturbation, and tabular evaluation are planned extensions. Agent evaluation scores a single-response model one turn at a time, relies on the caller to declare trial independence, treats alternative routes as whole routes, and validates tool calls against the common subset of JSON Schema.\nL458: Latency is sampled per generate() call, so a single-call local run reports one whole-run wall-clock sample rather than per-request latency (Section cite24†4.3 ); time-to-first-token and inter-token latency are outside the current scope, and the local hf: backend does not record token usage.\nL459: Red-teaming support is intentionally basic compared with Promptfoo [cite70†33 ], PyRIT [cite71†26 ], and DeepTeam [cite85†8 ], and its probes are dual-use and intended for authorized evaluation of models the user may test (Section cite7†2 ).\nL460: The case studies show what the library reports, and a few of their features are worth keeping in mind. They are small. They use a single judge (Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct, greedy-decoded). The library can use other supported backends as judges, but each study ran one judge configuration, so judged verdicts have not been validated against human annotation and may favor a candidate from the judge’s own model family (Study 3); the judge prompt is in Appendix cite59†A.6 .\nL461: Significance statements in Study 2 use the reported accuracies (95% Wilson intervals and a two-proportion test) because per-sample paired scores were not retained.\nL462: This paper contains no harmful content, adversarial prompts, or personally identifiable information, and the agent harness runs tools only through caller-supplied doubles. AuditKIT is released under the Lexsi Labs Source Available License v1.2, which is not an OSI-approved open-source licence and conditions commercial use; see the licence for its terms.\nL463: ## 10  Conclusion\nL464: \nL465: We presented AuditKIT, a source-available library that evaluates language models and agents through one run and comparison interface. It combines direction-aware metrics, configuration tracking, and extensible components with structured assessment of tool use, retrieval, and task outcomes. For agents, it keeps turn-level execution information, separates trajectory diagnostics from oracle-based outcomes, and reports when the available evidence is insufficient for scoring.\nL466: The seven case studies illustrate how evaluation choices shape reported results. Answer extraction changed the apparent model ranking while the generated responses stayed the same, serving-runtime differences changed exact-match outcomes, and retrieval metrics separated retrievers that hit rate treated as tied. These observations show why evaluation context should be retained and why each metric should be read together with the evidence it was computed from.\nL467: #### Availability.\nL468: \nL469: AuditKIT is available with documentation, a CLI, and examples (cite138†https://github.com/Lexsi-Labs/AuditKIT†github.com ) under the Lexsi Labs Source Available License (LSAL) v1.2.\nL470: ## References\nL471:   * [1] Arize AI (2024) Phoenix: ai observability and evaluation. External Links: cite139†Link†phoenix.arize.com Cited by: cite140†§2.1 .\nL472:   * [2] S. Bhattacharjee, K. Sharma, V. K. Sankarapu, and P. Seth (2026) CuratorKIT: data curation and synthetic data generation for LLM post-training. External Links: 2606.21631, cite141†Link†github.com Cited by: cite142†5th item , cite143†§7.5 .\nL473:   * [3] Braintrust Data, Inc. (2024) Braintrust. External Links: cite144†Link†www.braintrust.dev Cited by: cite145†§1 , cite140†§2.1 .\nL474:   * [4] M. Chen, J. Tworek, H. Jun, Q. Yuan, H. P. d. O. Pinto, J. Kaplan, et al. (2021) Evaluating large language models trained on code. arXiv preprint arXiv:2107.03374. Cited by: cite146†§7.3 .\nL475:   * [5] C. Clark, K. Lee, M. Chang, T. Kwiatkowski, M. Collins, and K. Toutanova (2019) BoolQ: exploring the surprising difficulty of natural yes/no questions. In Proceedings of the 2019 Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies, Volume 1 (Long and Short Papers), J. Burstein, C. Doran, and T. Solorio (Eds.), Minneapolis, Minnesota, pp. 2924–2936.\nL476: External Links: cite147†Link†aclanthology.org , cite148†Document†dx.doi.org Cited by: cite149†§8.1 .\nL477:   * [6] Cohe